lesson "Laws of Logarithms" {
  course: "pure-maths"
  unit: "Exponentials & Logarithms"
  difficulty: "Intermediate"
  icon: "Sigma"
  summary: "Logs turn multiplication into addition, which is the trick that once powered slide rules. Three laws, all inherited straight from the index laws."
  skills: ["explog-log-laws", "explog-power-law", "explog-common-errors"]

  slide "Multiplication Becomes Addition" {
    id: "e3-hook"
    cat: "The Three Laws"
    > Logs are powers, so the log laws are just the index laws in translation.
    >
    > Multiplying powers **adds** the exponents. A log *is* an exponent. So:
    >
    > $$\log(ab) = \log a + \log b$$
    >
    > Dividing subtracts, and a power of a power multiplies:
    >
    > $$\log\!\left(\frac{a}{b}\right) = \log a - \log b \qquad \log(a^n) = n\log a$$
    >
    > Three laws, and each is an index law you already met, seen from the other side.
    > That third one is the workhorse — it is what pulls an unknown down out of an
    > exponent, and the whole of the next lesson depends on it.

    match {
      ask "Match each expression to what it equals."
      skill: "explog-log-laws"
      pair "$\log 6 + \log 5$" -> "$\log 30$"
      pair "$\log 12 - \log 4$" -> "$\log 3$"
      pair "$3\log 2$" -> "$\log 8$"
      pair "$\tfrac12 \log 9$" -> "$\log 3$ "
      hint "Addition of logs is multiplication inside; a multiple in front is a power inside."
      ! "$\tfrac12\log 9 = \log 9^{1/2} = \log 3$, since a half power is a square root. The power law works for fractional powers just as happily as whole ones."
    }
  }

  slide "Why It Was Revolutionary" {
    id: "e3-history"
    cat: "The Three Laws"
    > It is easy to see logs as an exam topic. They were, for three hundred years,
    > the most important computational tool in existence.
    >
    > Multiplying two seven-digit numbers by hand is miserable and error-prone.
    > **Adding** two seven-digit numbers is routine. So astronomers and navigators
    > looked up the logs of their numbers, added them, then looked up the answer
    > backwards. Multiplication was reduced to addition.
    >
    > That is exactly what a slide rule does mechanically, and it is why log tables
    > sat in every scientist's desk until the pocket calculator arrived. The law
    > $\log(ab) = \log a + \log b$ was not a curiosity. It was infrastructure.

    numeric {
      ask "Given $\log 2 \approx 0.301$ and $\log 3 \approx 0.477$, what is $\log 6$?"
      skill: "explog-log-laws"
      answer: 0.778
      expect: 0.301 + 0.477
      tolerance: 0.002
      hint "$6 = 2 \times 3$, and multiplying inside means adding outside."
      ! "$0.301 + 0.477 = 0.778$. Two lookups and one addition replaced a multiplication, which is precisely how the tables were used."
    }
  }

  slide "The Error That Costs Most Marks" {
    id: "e3-trap"
    cat: "What Logs Do Not Do"
    quiz {
      ask "Is $\log(a + b)$ the same as $\log a + \log b$?"
      skill: "explog-common-errors"
      * "No — $\log a + \log b$ is $\log(ab)$, and $\log(a+b)$ does not simplify at all"
      - "Yes, logs distribute over addition" { why: "They emphatically do not. Test it: $\log(2+2) = \log 4 \approx 0.602$, but $\log 2 + \log 2 \approx 0.602$... which happens to match here by coincidence, since $2+2 = 2\times2$. Try $3$ and $4$ instead and the two are nowhere near." }
      - "Only when $a$ and $b$ are positive" { why: "Positivity is required for the logs to exist at all, but it does not make the false law true." }
      hint "Which operation *inside* a log corresponds to addition outside it?"
      onwrong: "e3-no-distribution" retry
      ! "**Adding logs multiplies the insides.** There is no law at all for the log of a sum, and $\log(a+b)$ simply stays as it is."
    }
  }

  slide "Logs Do Not Distribute" {
    id: "e3-no-distribution"
    hidden: true
    cat: "What Logs Do Not Do"
    > This is worth testing with real numbers once, so you never write it again.
    >
    > Take $a = 3$ and $b = 4$, using base $10$.
    >
    > $$\log(3 + 4) = \log 7 \approx 0.845$$
    > $$\log 3 + \log 4 \approx 0.477 + 0.602 = 1.079$$
    >
    > Nowhere near. And that second number is not meaningless — it is $\log 12$,
    > because adding logs multiplies the insides, and $3 \times 4 = 12$.
    >
    > Two others in the same family, both false:
    >
    > $$\dfrac{\log a}{\log b} \neq \log\!\left(\dfrac{a}{b}\right) \qquad (\log a)^2 \neq 2\log a$$
    >
    > The first is a genuinely useful expression, but it equals $\log_b a$, not the
    > log of a quotient. The second confuses squaring the log with the log of a
    > square.
    >
    > **The rule of thumb: a log law only ever exists when the operation is inside
    > the log.**

    numeric {
      ask "Using $\log 3 \approx 0.477$ and $\log 4 \approx 0.602$, what is $\log 12$?"
      skill: "explog-log-laws"
      answer: 1.079
      expect: 0.477 + 0.602
      tolerance: 0.002
      hint "$12 = 3 \times 4$, so add the logs."
      ! "$1.079$. Adding the logs gave the log of the *product*, which is exactly what the law promises."
    }
  }

  slide "Condensing and Expanding" {
    id: "e3-condense"
    cat: "Using the Laws"
    > Questions run both ways. Sometimes you expand one log into several; more often
    > you **condense** several into one, because a single log can then be undone in
    > one step.
    >
    > $$2\log x + \log 3 - \log 5 = \log x^2 + \log 3 - \log 5 = \log\!\left(\frac{3x^2}{5}\right)$$
    >
    > The order that always works: **powers first**, using the multiple-in-front law
    > to move coefficients up as exponents. Then combine the additions into
    > multiplications and the subtractions into divisions.
    >
    > Condense before you solve. Trying to solve while several logs are still
    > floating about is how questions become hard.

    order {
      ask "Put the steps in order to condense $3\log a - 2\log b$ into a single logarithm."
      skill: "explog-power-law"
      item "Move the coefficients up as powers: $\log a^3 - \log b^2$"
      item "Recognise the subtraction as a division"
      item "Combine into one log: $\log\!\left(\dfrac{a^3}{b^2}\right)$"
      decoy "Divide the coefficients to get $\tfrac32 \log(a - b)$"
      ! "Powers first, then combine. The decoy invents a law that does not exist, and it does it by treating the log as if it distributed."
    }
  }

  slide "Change of Base" {
    id: "e3-change"
    cat: "Using the Laws"
    > Your calculator usually offers only $\log_{10}$ and $\ln$. For any other base
    > there is one more formula:
    >
    > $$\log_a b = \frac{\log b}{\log a}$$
    >
    > and you may use either base on the right, as long as you use the same one top
    > and bottom.
    >
    > So $\log_2 10 = \dfrac{\log 10}{\log 2} = \dfrac{1}{0.301} \approx 3.32$, which
    > makes sense: $2^3 = 8$ and $2^4 = 16$, so the answer should sit between $3$ and
    > $4$.
    >
    > This is also the correct home for that $\dfrac{\log a}{\log b}$ expression from
    > earlier. It is not the log of a quotient — it is a change of base.

    numeric {
      ask "Using $\log 2 \approx 0.301$ and $\log 5 \approx 0.699$, evaluate $\log_2 5$."
      skill: "explog-log-laws"
      answer: 2.322
      expect: 0.699/0.301
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "$\log_2 5 = \dfrac{\log 5}{\log 2}$."
      ! "$0.699/0.301 \approx 2.32$. Sanity check: $2^2 = 4$ and $2^3 = 8$, so the power that gives $5$ must lie between $2$ and $3$."
    }
  }

  slide "All Three at Once" {
    id: "e3-synth"
    cat: "Using the Laws"
    numeric {
      ask "Simplify $\log_2 40 - \log_2 5$."
      skill: "explog-log-laws"
      answer: 3
      expect: log2(40/5)
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "Subtracting logs of the same base means dividing the insides: $40/5$."
      ! "$\log_2 8 = 3$. Combining first turned an awkward pair into a log you can do in your head — which is why condensing is almost always the right first move."
    }
  }
}
